        <div><dt>Gaming and technology</dt><dd>Studios and Surrey Research Park firms competing nationally on complex sites.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Professional services</dt><dd>Solicitors, accountants and advisers competing for high-intent local searches.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Property and construction</dt><dd>Estate agents and developers targeting location and buying-intent queries.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Healthcare</dt><dd>Practices where health content needs real expertise and patient trust signals.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="proof-strip-tools" style="border-top:0;padding-top:0"><span>Areas:</span><span class="proof-chip">Guildford</span><span class="proof-chip">Onslow Village</span><span class="proof-chip">Burpham</span><span class="proof-chip">Merrow</span><span class="proof-chip">Stoughton</span><span class="proof-chip">Park Barn</span><span class="proof-chip">Bellfields</span><span class="proof-chip">Westborough</span><span class="proof-chip">Pewley Hill</span><span class="proof-chip">Charlotteville</span><span class="proof-chip">Shalford</span><span class="proof-chip">Woking</span><span class="proof-chip">Godalming</span><span class="proof-chip">Farnham</span><span class="proof-chip">Camberley</span><span class="proof-chip">Cobham</span><span class="proof-chip">Esher</span><span class="proof-chip">Weybridge</span></p>
      <p class="mt-4"><a class="cta-link" href="/areas/">See all areas I cover &rarr;</a> <a class="cta-link" style="margin-left:1.5rem" href="/industries/">Sector detail &rarr;</a> <a class="cta-link" style="margin-left:1.5rem" href="/about/">About me &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="doc">
    <div class="prose">
    <div >
      <h2>Results that matter</h2>

      <p>I separate search visibility from commercial outcomes instead of treating every ranking gain as revenue. The <a href="/case-studies/">results page</a> shows dated, anonymised portfolio data from Google Search Console and GA4, with the limits of that evidence stated alongside the charts.</p>

      <p>Every client receives monthly reporting with clear, substantive metrics: organic sessions, keyword movements, conversion rates, and projected revenue impact. I set expectations candidly and hold myself accountable to them.</p>
    </div>

    <div >
      <h2>The Guildford SEO landscape</h2>

      <p>Search behaviour differs by type of business. A solicitor on Castle Street needs to rank for "solicitor guildford" in the local pack. A SaaS company at Surrey Research Park needs to rank for industry terms nationally. A restaurant near the Yvonne Arnaud Theatre needs to appear in "near me" searches from theatregoers making last-minute decisions. One generic approach to SEO does not suit all of these. I plan each business separately.</p>

      <p>AI search adds another channel. Google AI Overviews now appear for many local queries. ChatGPT and Perplexity are becoming meaningful discovery tools for high-consideration purchases. Businesses that ignore AI search risk losing ground to competitors who prepare for it.</p>
    </div>
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>


    <div class="not-prose reveal">
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How much does SEO cost in Guildford?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>SEO services in Guildford typically range from £500 to £3,500 per month. The price depends on competition, the number of target keywords and the scope of work. Guildford's affluent market and its competitive industries, technology, property, and professional services among them, often require sustained investment before meaningful results materialise. See my <a href="/seo-pricing-guildford/">pricing page</a> for detailed options.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

